2025-05-01 03:30:21 - Thread-1457 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used in the real solution

player_match  
• match_id : links a player to a particular match  
• player_id : the player that appeared in that match  
• team_id   : needed only to know which side the player batted for  

ball_by_ball  
• match_id  : ties every delivery to its match  
• over_id , ball_id : chronological order of the deliveries – indispensable to delimit a partnership (from wicket to wicket)  
• innings_no: we need partnerships inside each innings  
• striker , non_striker : the two players who are in the partnership on that delivery  
• bowler    : not directly needed, but can be handy for checking joins  

batsman_scored  
• match_id , over_id , ball_id : to match with ball‑by‑ball rows  
• runs_scored : runs that are credited to the striker (individual runs)  
• innings_no  : same innings filter  

extra_runs  
• match_id , over_id , ball_id , innings_no : align with the delivery  
• extra_runs : add to the partnership’s total even though they are not credited to either batsman  

wicket_taken  
• match_id , over_id , ball_id , player_out , innings_no : signals the end of a partnership; player_out tells which of the two got out  

match  
• match_id : final grouping key – we want “best partnership per match”  

player  
• player_id , player_name : just for human readable output at the very end  


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------  
Exploratory / scaffolding queries  
(The queries get progressively closer to the final computation; each uses LIMIT 20.)

```sql
-- 1. List a few matches that have ball‑by‑ball data
SELECT DISTINCT match_id
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek at how striker / non‑striker look for a single match
SELECT over_id, ball_id, striker, non_striker
FROM ball_by_ball
WHERE match_id = (SELECT match_id FROM ball_by_ball LIMIT 1)
ORDER BY over_id, ball_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Show one delivery with the batsman runs already joined in
SELECT b.match_id, b.over_id, b.ball_id,
       b.striker, b.non_striker,
       s.runs_scored
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON  s.match_id = b.match_id
  AND s.over_id  = b.over_id
  AND s.ball_id  = b.ball_id
  AND s.innings_no = b.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Add any extras on the same delivery
SELECT b.match_id, b.over_id, b.ball_id,
       COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) AS bat_runs,
       COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0) AS extra_runs
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON  s.match_id = b.match_id
  AND s.over_id  = b.over_id
  AND s.ball_id  = b.ball_id
  AND s.innings_no = b.innings_no
LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
  ON  e.match_id = b.match_id
  AND e.over_id  = b.over_id
  AND e.ball_id  = b.ball_id
  AND e.innings_no = b.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Count how many wicket records accompany deliveries
SELECT w.match_id, COUNT(*) AS wicket_events
FROM wicket_taken AS w
GROUP BY w.match_id
ORDER BY wicket_events DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Identify, for one innings, the deliveries where a wicket fell
SELECT w.match_id, w.innings_no, w.over_id, w.ball_id, w.player_out
FROM wicket_taken AS w
WHERE w.match_id = (SELECT match_id FROM wicket_taken LIMIT 1)
ORDER BY w.over_id, w.ball_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Aggregate total runs made by each individual batsman per match & innings
SELECT b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.striker AS player_id,
       SUM(COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0)) AS individual_runs
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON  s.match_id = b.match_id
  AND s.over_id  = b.over_id
  AND s.ball_id  = b.ball_id
  AND s.innings_no = b.innings_no
GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.striker
ORDER BY individual_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Naïve (no wicket boundary) partnership runs: sum striker + non‑striker per delivery
SELECT b.match_id, b.innings_no,
       b.striker , b.non_striker,
       SUM(COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0)) AS partnership_runs
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON  s.match_id = b.match_id
  AND s.over_id  = b.over_id
  AND s.ball_id  = b.ball_id
  AND s.innings_no = b.innings_no
LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
  ON  e.match_id = b.match_id
  AND e.over_id  = b.over_id
  AND e.ball_id  = b.ball_id
  AND e.innings_no = b.innings_no
GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.striker, b.non_striker
ORDER BY partnership_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Pull the two highest‑scoring partnerships (still naïve) in each match
WITH pair_runs AS (
  SELECT b.match_id,
         CASE WHEN b.striker > b.non_striker THEN b.striker ELSE b.non_striker END AS p1,
         CASE WHEN b.striker < b.non_striker THEN b.striker ELSE b.non_striker END AS p2,
         SUM(COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0)) AS pair_total
  FROM ball_by_ball AS b
  LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
    ON s.match_id = b.match_id
   AND s.over_id  = b.over_id
   AND s.ball_id  = b.ball_id
   AND s.innings_no = b.innings_no
  LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
    ON e.match_id = b.match_id
   AND e.over_id  = b.over_id
   AND e.ball_id  = b.ball_id
   AND e.innings_no = b.innings_no
  GROUP BY b.match_id, p1, p2
), ranked AS (
  SELECT pr.*,
         RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY pr.match_id ORDER BY pr.pair_total DESC) AS rk
  FROM pair_runs AS pr
)
SELECT *
FROM ranked
WHERE rk <= 2
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Bring individual contributions for the top partnership of each match
WITH pair_runs AS (
  SELECT b.match_id,
         b.striker AS p1,
         b.non_striker AS p2,
         SUM(COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0) + COALESCE(e.extra_runs,0)) AS pair_total
  FROM ball_by_ball AS b
  LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
    ON s.match_id = b.match_id
   AND s.over_id  = b.over_id
   AND s.ball_id  = b.ball_id
   AND s.innings_no = b.innings_no
  LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
    ON e.match_id = b.match_id
   AND e.over_id  = b.over_id
   AND e.ball_id  = b.ball_id
   AND e.innings_no = b.innings_no
  GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker, b.non_striker
), best_pair AS (
  SELECT *
  FROM (
    SELECT pr.*,
           RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY pr.match_id ORDER BY pr.pair_total DESC) AS rk
    FROM pair_runs AS pr
  )
  WHERE rk = 1
), individual AS (
  SELECT b.match_id, b.striker AS player_id,
         SUM(COALESCE(s.runs_scored,0)) AS indiv_runs
  FROM ball_by_ball AS b
  LEFT JOIN batsman_scored AS s
    ON s.match_id = b.match_id
   AND s.over_id  = b.over_id
   AND s.ball_id  = b.ball_id
   AND s.innings_no = b.innings_no
  GROUP BY b.match_id, b.striker
)
SELECT bp.match_id,
       CASE
         WHEN i1.indiv_runs > i2.indiv_runs THEN bp.p1
         WHEN i1.indiv_runs < i2.indiv_runs THEN bp.p2
         WHEN bp.p1 > bp.p2                THEN bp.p1
         ELSE bp.p2
       END AS player1_id,
       CASE
         WHEN i1.indiv_runs > i2.indiv_runs THEN bp.p2
         WHEN i1.indiv_runs < i2.indiv_runs THEN bp.p1
         WHEN bp.p1 > bp.p2                THEN bp.p2
         ELSE bp.p1
       END AS player2_id,
       MAX(i1.indiv_runs, i2.indiv_runs) AS player1_runs,
       MIN(i1.indiv_runs, i2.indiv_runs) AS player2_runs,
       bp.pair_total                      AS partnership_runs
FROM best_pair AS bp
JOIN individual AS i1
  ON i1.match_id = bp.match_id AND i1.player_id = bp.p1
JOIN individual AS i2
  ON i2.match_id = bp.match_id AND i2.player_id = bp.p2
LIMIT 20;
```

(The later CTE‑based statements purposely ignore wickets; they are shown only to illustrate how one would keep refining the query toward the final “highest partnership per match” requirement.)
